1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,373
Sit down, sweetheart,

2
00:00:01,373 --> 00:00:03,663
and don't tell your mother what I'm

3
00:00:03,663 --> 00:00:04,841
about to tell you.

4
00:00:05,021 --> 00:00:07,291
Because some of the things we did

5
00:00:07,291 --> 00:00:08,529
when I was a girl,

6
00:00:08,529 --> 00:00:11,488
well, today somebody would call the police.

7
00:00:11,668 --> 00:00:13,096
I'm not joking.

8
00:00:13,276 --> 00:00:15,183
Your mother would lose you to the

9
00:00:15,183 --> 00:00:17,264
county for half the things my mother

10
00:00:17,264 --> 00:00:18,593
did to me before lunch.

11
00:00:18,773 --> 00:00:20,920
And we thought it was just a

12
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:22,070
normal Tuesday.

13
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:24,496
I grew up in Mansfield,

14
00:00:24,496 --> 00:00:27,204
Ohio, in the nineteen fifties.

15
00:00:27,384 --> 00:00:29,306
I was born in forty-four,

16
00:00:29,306 --> 00:00:31,228
so by the time I was ten,

17
00:00:31,228 --> 00:00:33,535
the whole country was booming,

18
00:00:33,535 --> 00:00:35,841
and nobody, and I mean nobody,

19
00:00:35,841 --> 00:00:37,763
was worried about safety.

20
00:00:37,943 --> 00:00:39,847
We didn't even have the word.

21
00:00:40,027 --> 00:00:42,059
Let me count them off for you.

22
00:00:42,239 --> 00:00:43,562
You'll see.

23
00:00:43,913 --> 00:00:45,039
Number one.

24
00:00:45,219 --> 00:00:46,089
The car.

25
00:00:46,269 --> 00:00:49,242
Our Chevrolet had no seat belts.

26
00:00:49,422 --> 00:00:51,082
Not broken ones.

27
00:00:51,262 --> 00:00:52,156
None.

28
00:00:52,336 --> 00:00:54,802
They didn't even put them in most

29
00:00:54,802 --> 00:00:57,118
cars until nineteen sixty-four,

30
00:00:57,118 --> 00:00:59,584
and nobody had to wear them until

31
00:00:59,584 --> 00:01:00,556
the eighties.

32
00:01:00,736 --> 00:01:02,882
When Father stopped short,

33
00:01:02,882 --> 00:01:06,018
Mother's arm shot out across my chest.

34
00:01:06,198 --> 00:01:07,986
That was the seat belt.

35
00:01:08,166 --> 00:01:09,153
Her arm.

36
00:01:09,503 --> 00:01:10,873
And the babies.

37
00:01:11,053 --> 00:01:12,167
Oh, honey.

38
00:01:12,347 --> 00:01:14,871
Babies rode on their mother's lap in

39
00:01:14,871 --> 00:01:15,923
the front seat.

40
00:01:16,103 --> 00:01:18,060
Or in a little canvas seat that

41
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:20,207
hooked over the back of the bench,

42
00:01:20,207 --> 00:01:22,038
so the baby could see out the

43
00:01:22,038 --> 00:01:22,732
windshield.

44
00:01:22,912 --> 00:01:25,461
My brother Danny rode in one of

45
00:01:25,461 --> 00:01:25,954
those.

46
00:01:26,134 --> 00:01:28,393
It was made to keep him busy,

47
00:01:28,393 --> 00:01:29,095
not safe.

48
00:01:29,275 --> 00:01:31,468
Today that would be a crime in

49
00:01:31,468 --> 00:01:32,711
all fifty states.

50
00:01:33,062 --> 00:01:34,950
In the summer we rode in the

51
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:37,311
back of Uncle Frank's pickup truck.

52
00:01:37,491 --> 00:01:38,612
Six of us kids,

53
00:01:38,612 --> 00:01:40,630
sitting on the wheel wells,

54
00:01:40,630 --> 00:01:42,947
hair flying, all the way out to

55
00:01:42,947 --> 00:01:45,339
the county fair on Route thirty.

56
00:01:45,519 --> 00:01:47,864
Nobody held on to anything.

57
00:01:48,044 --> 00:01:49,774
We sang the whole way.

58
00:01:49,954 --> 00:01:52,029
If a policeman passed us,

59
00:01:52,029 --> 00:01:52,775
he waved.

60
00:01:53,126 --> 00:01:55,795
And on long trips Father smoked with

61
00:01:55,795 --> 00:01:57,723
all the windows rolled up.

62
00:01:57,903 --> 00:01:59,029
Mother too.

63
00:01:59,209 --> 00:02:01,112
The car would fill up blue,

64
00:02:01,112 --> 00:02:03,367
and Danny and I would draw faces

65
00:02:03,367 --> 00:02:05,270
in the smoke on the window.

66
00:02:05,450 --> 00:02:07,022
When we got carsick,

67
00:02:07,022 --> 00:02:09,223
Mother said it was the road.

68
00:02:09,573 --> 00:02:11,871
Which brings me to number two.

69
00:02:12,051 --> 00:02:13,108
Cigarettes.

70
00:02:13,288 --> 00:02:15,842
Everybody smoked, sweetheart.

71
00:02:16,022 --> 00:02:17,160
Everybody.

72
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:19,859
Doctors smoked in the examining room.

73
00:02:20,039 --> 00:02:23,137
There were advertisements that said more doctors

74
00:02:23,137 --> 00:02:25,589
smoke Camels than any other cigarette.

75
00:02:25,769 --> 00:02:28,393
My teacher smoked at her desk during

76
00:02:28,393 --> 00:02:29,704
the spelling test.

77
00:02:29,884 --> 00:02:32,398
There were ashtrays on the airplane,

78
00:02:32,398 --> 00:02:33,515
in the hospital,

79
00:02:33,515 --> 00:02:35,121
in the church basement.

80
00:02:35,471 --> 00:02:37,432
And here is the part you won't

81
00:02:37,432 --> 00:02:37,955
believe.

82
00:02:38,135 --> 00:02:39,316
When I was nine,

83
00:02:39,316 --> 00:02:41,825
Father would give me a quarter and

84
00:02:41,825 --> 00:02:44,040
send me to Rexall Drugs to buy

85
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:45,368
his Lucky Strikes.

86
00:02:45,548 --> 00:02:48,288
A pack cost about twenty-three cents.

87
00:02:48,468 --> 00:02:51,233
The man behind the counter knew whose

88
00:02:51,233 --> 00:02:52,055
girl I was,

89
00:02:52,055 --> 00:02:54,072
and he gave me the pack and

90
00:02:54,072 --> 00:02:55,492
two pennies change.

91
00:02:55,672 --> 00:02:58,540
Nobody asked a single question.

92
00:02:58,890 --> 00:03:01,548
We even had candy cigarettes.

93
00:03:01,728 --> 00:03:04,258
Little white sticks of sugar with a

94
00:03:04,258 --> 00:03:06,353
red tip, in a box that looked

95
00:03:06,353 --> 00:03:08,160
just like the real thing.

96
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:10,877
We held them between our fingers and

97
00:03:10,877 --> 00:03:13,344
blew pretend smoke in the cold air.

98
00:03:13,524 --> 00:03:15,232
We were practicing,

99
00:03:15,232 --> 00:03:15,951
you see.

100
00:03:16,131 --> 00:03:17,292
And it worked.

101
00:03:17,472 --> 00:03:20,037
Half of my class was smoking by

102
00:03:20,037 --> 00:03:20,699
sixteen.

103
00:03:21,049 --> 00:03:22,256
Number three.

104
00:03:22,436 --> 00:03:24,096
They left us alone.

105
00:03:24,276 --> 00:03:25,344
All day.

106
00:03:25,524 --> 00:03:28,127
In the summer Mother opened the screen

107
00:03:28,127 --> 00:03:30,182
door after breakfast and said,

108
00:03:30,182 --> 00:03:32,168
go play, and be home when the

109
00:03:32,168 --> 00:03:33,675
street lights come on.

110
00:03:33,855 --> 00:03:35,840
That was the only rule.

111
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:39,142
Nobody knew where we were for eight

112
00:03:39,142 --> 00:03:39,677
hours.

113
00:03:39,857 --> 00:03:42,968
No phone, no way to find us.

114
00:03:43,148 --> 00:03:44,820
And they didn't worry.

115
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,974
They had laundry to do.

116
00:03:47,324 --> 00:03:49,613
We played in the creek behind the

117
00:03:49,613 --> 00:03:50,307
rail yard.

118
00:03:50,487 --> 00:03:52,947
We climbed the water tower once,

119
00:03:52,947 --> 00:03:55,176
and I will never tell you how

120
00:03:55,176 --> 00:03:55,561
high.

121
00:03:55,741 --> 00:03:57,745
We played in a house that was

122
00:03:57,745 --> 00:04:00,371
being built, walking on the beams with

123
00:04:00,371 --> 00:04:01,616
no floor under us.

124
00:04:01,796 --> 00:04:03,689
When the workmen saw us,

125
00:04:03,689 --> 00:04:05,662
they didn't call anybody.

126
00:04:05,842 --> 00:04:08,177
They yelled, get down from there,

127
00:04:08,177 --> 00:04:10,230
and went back to their lunch.

128
00:04:10,581 --> 00:04:13,850
And sweetheart, I started babysitting when I

129
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:14,667
was eleven.

130
00:04:14,847 --> 00:04:16,168
Eleven years old,

131
00:04:16,168 --> 00:04:18,811
alone in a stranger's house with a

132
00:04:18,811 --> 00:04:20,288
baby and a toddler,

133
00:04:20,288 --> 00:04:21,453
until midnight.

134
00:04:21,633 --> 00:04:23,270
Fifty cents an hour.

135
00:04:23,450 --> 00:04:25,821
The parents went to the Elks Club

136
00:04:25,821 --> 00:04:28,478
and came home smelling of whiskey and

137
00:04:28,478 --> 00:04:31,136
drove the babysitter home themselves.

138
00:04:31,316 --> 00:04:33,940
Today they'd lose those children.

139
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:36,245
Back then they were good neighbors.

140
00:04:36,595 --> 00:04:39,278
Mothers left babies outside the store in

141
00:04:39,278 --> 00:04:41,424
the carriage while they shopped.

142
00:04:41,604 --> 00:04:44,220
Just parked them on the sidewalk,

143
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:45,488
in the sunshine,

144
00:04:45,488 --> 00:04:47,866
while they went into the A and

145
00:04:47,866 --> 00:04:48,025
P.

146
00:04:48,205 --> 00:04:49,952
And if you were a kid,

147
00:04:49,952 --> 00:04:51,699
you waited in the car.

148
00:04:51,879 --> 00:04:53,145
In July.

149
00:04:53,325 --> 00:04:55,891
With the windows cracked an inch.

150
00:04:56,071 --> 00:04:57,382
For an hour.

151
00:04:57,562 --> 00:04:59,919
That was called being good.

152
00:05:00,270 --> 00:05:02,670
Number four is the one that still

153
00:05:02,670 --> 00:05:03,834
gives me chills.

154
00:05:04,014 --> 00:05:06,623
Every summer evening a truck came down

155
00:05:06,623 --> 00:05:09,164
our street spraying a big white cloud

156
00:05:09,164 --> 00:05:10,469
for the mosquitoes.

157
00:05:10,649 --> 00:05:12,425
And we ran behind it.

158
00:05:12,605 --> 00:05:14,382
All the children did.

159
00:05:14,562 --> 00:05:16,659
We ran right into that fog,

160
00:05:16,659 --> 00:05:20,389
laughing, spinning, playing hide and seek inside

161
00:05:20,389 --> 00:05:20,622
it.

162
00:05:20,802 --> 00:05:22,279
It smelled sweet,

163
00:05:22,279 --> 00:05:23,496
like kerosene.

164
00:05:23,846 --> 00:05:25,416
It was DDT,

165
00:05:25,416 --> 00:05:26,272
honey.

166
00:05:26,452 --> 00:05:28,672
They sprayed it on beaches,

167
00:05:28,672 --> 00:05:30,891
on children at summer camp,

168
00:05:30,891 --> 00:05:31,549
on cows.

169
00:05:31,729 --> 00:05:34,010
Mother put it on the shelves to

170
00:05:34,010 --> 00:05:36,071
keep the bugs off the flour.

171
00:05:36,251 --> 00:05:38,933
Then a woman named Rachel Carson wrote

172
00:05:38,933 --> 00:05:41,896
a book in nineteen sixty-two called Silent

173
00:05:41,896 --> 00:05:44,578
Spring, and she told everybody what it

174
00:05:44,578 --> 00:05:45,284
was doing.

175
00:05:45,464 --> 00:05:47,714
It was banned in this country in

176
00:05:47,714 --> 00:05:49,190
nineteen seventy-two.

177
00:05:49,370 --> 00:05:51,978
We had already breathed it for twenty

178
00:05:51,978 --> 00:05:52,401
years.

179
00:05:52,751 --> 00:05:55,811
And the paint on my bedroom windowsill,

180
00:05:55,811 --> 00:05:57,381
the pretty pink one,

181
00:05:57,381 --> 00:05:58,950
that had lead in it.

182
00:05:59,130 --> 00:06:01,081
Gasoline had lead in it.

183
00:06:01,261 --> 00:06:03,302
Our thermometers had mercury,

184
00:06:03,302 --> 00:06:04,640
and when one broke,

185
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:07,456
we rolled the little silver balls around

186
00:06:07,456 --> 00:06:10,131
on the kitchen table with our fingers.

187
00:06:10,311 --> 00:06:11,890
They were so beautiful.

188
00:06:12,070 --> 00:06:13,254
Like magic.

189
00:06:13,434 --> 00:06:15,152
Don't you ever touch one.

190
00:06:15,503 --> 00:06:17,758
Number five sounds made up,

191
00:06:17,758 --> 00:06:19,763
but I promise it's true.

192
00:06:19,943 --> 00:06:22,392
At Kobacker's shoe store downtown,

193
00:06:22,392 --> 00:06:25,272
they had a machine called a fluoroscope.

194
00:06:25,452 --> 00:06:27,237
You put your feet in a slot

195
00:06:27,237 --> 00:06:28,163
at the bottom,

196
00:06:28,163 --> 00:06:30,410
and you looked through a window on

197
00:06:30,410 --> 00:06:32,525
top, and you could see the bones

198
00:06:32,525 --> 00:06:34,971
of your toes wiggling inside your new

199
00:06:34,971 --> 00:06:35,367
shoes.

200
00:06:35,547 --> 00:06:37,115
Green and glowing.

201
00:06:37,465 --> 00:06:39,074
It was an X-ray,

202
00:06:39,074 --> 00:06:40,181
sweetheart.

203
00:06:40,361 --> 00:06:41,481
A real one,

204
00:06:41,481 --> 00:06:43,416
with no lead apron,

205
00:06:43,416 --> 00:06:45,249
and no time limit.

206
00:06:45,429 --> 00:06:47,711
Danny and I would put our feet

207
00:06:47,711 --> 00:06:50,145
in it over and over while Mother

208
00:06:50,145 --> 00:06:51,362
tried on gloves.

209
00:06:51,542 --> 00:06:53,272
The salesman let us.

210
00:06:53,452 --> 00:06:55,019
It was a free show.

211
00:06:55,199 --> 00:06:58,553
They had thousands of those machines all

212
00:06:58,553 --> 00:07:01,907
over America, and most states didn't ban

213
00:07:01,907 --> 00:07:04,255
them until the late fifties.

214
00:07:04,605 --> 00:07:05,940
Number six.

215
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:07,350
The toys.

216
00:07:07,530 --> 00:07:09,790
Every boy on our street had a

217
00:07:09,790 --> 00:07:11,738
BB gun by the time he was

218
00:07:11,738 --> 00:07:12,128
nine.

219
00:07:12,308 --> 00:07:14,584
Danny got his Red Ryder for Christmas

220
00:07:14,584 --> 00:07:15,691
when he was eight,

221
00:07:15,691 --> 00:07:17,783
and he shot out the Pruitts' porch

222
00:07:17,783 --> 00:07:19,320
light the same afternoon.

223
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:21,880
Father took the gun away for one

224
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:22,252
week.

225
00:07:22,432 --> 00:07:23,709
One week.

226
00:07:23,889 --> 00:07:26,062
Then he gave it back and showed

227
00:07:26,062 --> 00:07:27,744
him how to aim properly.

228
00:07:28,094 --> 00:07:29,406
We had lawn darts,

229
00:07:29,406 --> 00:07:32,175
heavy metal spikes that you threw high

230
00:07:32,175 --> 00:07:34,071
up in the air and tried to

231
00:07:34,071 --> 00:07:35,164
land in a ring.

232
00:07:35,344 --> 00:07:38,395
We had chemistry sets with real chemicals,

233
00:07:38,395 --> 00:07:40,648
the kind that could burn a hole

234
00:07:40,648 --> 00:07:41,447
in the rug,

235
00:07:41,447 --> 00:07:43,554
and some of them had a little

236
00:07:43,554 --> 00:07:45,225
bit of uranium in them,

237
00:07:45,225 --> 00:07:47,186
for the atomic experiments.

238
00:07:47,366 --> 00:07:49,447
And for the Fourth of July,

239
00:07:49,447 --> 00:07:52,066
Uncle Frank bought a box of cherry

240
00:07:52,066 --> 00:07:54,994
bombs and let us light them ourselves.

241
00:07:55,344 --> 00:07:57,827
We roller skated with metal skates that

242
00:07:57,827 --> 00:07:59,992
clamped onto your shoes with a key

243
00:07:59,992 --> 00:08:01,648
you wore around your neck.

244
00:08:01,828 --> 00:08:03,117
No helmets.

245
00:08:03,297 --> 00:08:05,766
Nobody had heard of a helmet that

246
00:08:05,766 --> 00:08:08,236
wasn't for a football player or a

247
00:08:08,236 --> 00:08:08,834
soldier.

248
00:08:09,014 --> 00:08:10,158
When you fell,

249
00:08:10,158 --> 00:08:11,383
you got a scab.

250
00:08:11,563 --> 00:08:13,622
When the scab came off,

251
00:08:13,622 --> 00:08:14,965
you fell again.

252
00:08:15,315 --> 00:08:16,580
Number seven.

253
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:17,596
School.

254
00:08:17,776 --> 00:08:20,216
The principal at our school kept a

255
00:08:20,216 --> 00:08:22,225
wooden paddle in his office,

256
00:08:22,225 --> 00:08:24,377
with holes drilled in it so it

257
00:08:24,377 --> 00:08:25,740
would swing faster.

258
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:28,626
The boys got paddled for talking back.

259
00:08:28,806 --> 00:08:31,197
Nobody's parents complained.

260
00:08:31,377 --> 00:08:32,672
Most of the time,

261
00:08:32,672 --> 00:08:33,966
the parents said,

262
00:08:33,966 --> 00:08:36,327
good, and gave the boy a second

263
00:08:36,327 --> 00:08:37,240
one at home.

264
00:08:37,591 --> 00:08:40,133
And I'll tell you something else.

265
00:08:40,313 --> 00:08:42,215
We had a teacher who told a

266
00:08:42,215 --> 00:08:44,187
girl in my class she was too

267
00:08:44,187 --> 00:08:45,806
stupid for high school,

268
00:08:45,806 --> 00:08:47,778
right in front of everybody.

269
00:08:47,958 --> 00:08:49,571
Her name was Betty,

270
00:08:49,571 --> 00:08:51,778
and she cried at her desk.

271
00:08:51,958 --> 00:08:54,549
Some things about the old days were

272
00:08:54,549 --> 00:08:54,919
good.

273
00:08:55,099 --> 00:08:56,805
That wasn't one of them.

274
00:08:56,985 --> 00:09:00,176
Remember that when people tell you everything

275
00:09:00,176 --> 00:09:00,956
was better.

276
00:09:01,306 --> 00:09:02,281
Number eight.

277
00:09:02,461 --> 00:09:03,575
The medicine.

278
00:09:03,755 --> 00:09:05,516
When Mother felt tired,

279
00:09:05,516 --> 00:09:07,889
the doctor gave her diet pills.

280
00:09:08,069 --> 00:09:09,793
They were amphetamines,

281
00:09:09,793 --> 00:09:12,717
honey, the same thing they'd arrest you

282
00:09:12,717 --> 00:09:13,467
for today.

283
00:09:13,647 --> 00:09:15,906
Half the mothers on our street took

284
00:09:15,906 --> 00:09:17,455
them, with their coffee,

285
00:09:17,455 --> 00:09:19,715
and cleaned the whole house by nine

286
00:09:19,715 --> 00:09:20,683
in the morning.

287
00:09:20,863 --> 00:09:22,782
They called them pep pills,

288
00:09:22,782 --> 00:09:24,346
like it was a vitamin.

289
00:09:24,696 --> 00:09:26,186
When I had a cough,

290
00:09:26,186 --> 00:09:28,853
Mother gave me a spoonful of syrup

291
00:09:28,853 --> 00:09:30,658
that had codeine in it,

292
00:09:30,658 --> 00:09:32,148
from the drugstore,

293
00:09:32,148 --> 00:09:33,403
no prescription.

294
00:09:33,583 --> 00:09:35,324
When I had a toothache,

295
00:09:35,324 --> 00:09:37,670
they rubbed whiskey on my gums.

296
00:09:37,850 --> 00:09:40,466
And when Danny couldn't sleep as a

297
00:09:40,466 --> 00:09:43,004
baby, Grandma Pearl put a drop of

298
00:09:43,004 --> 00:09:44,851
paregoric in his bottle.

299
00:09:45,031 --> 00:09:46,343
That was opium.

300
00:09:46,523 --> 00:09:48,287
In a baby bottle.

301
00:09:48,467 --> 00:09:50,847
And she was a churchgoing woman.

302
00:09:51,198 --> 00:09:52,277
Number nine.

303
00:09:52,457 --> 00:09:54,582
The rules for teenagers.

304
00:09:54,762 --> 00:09:57,547
In Ohio, when I turned eighteen in

305
00:09:57,547 --> 00:10:00,741
nineteen sixty-two, I could walk into a

306
00:10:00,741 --> 00:10:02,297
bar and buy a beer.

307
00:10:02,477 --> 00:10:03,731
It was a weak one,

308
00:10:03,731 --> 00:10:05,681
called three point two beer,

309
00:10:05,681 --> 00:10:06,865
but it was legal.

310
00:10:07,045 --> 00:10:09,275
And nobody checked anything.

311
00:10:09,455 --> 00:10:11,424
Some of the boys in my class

312
00:10:11,424 --> 00:10:13,746
had been buying it since fifteen,

313
00:10:13,746 --> 00:10:15,364
with a pencil mustache.

314
00:10:15,714 --> 00:10:18,117
People hitchhiked, sweetheart.

315
00:10:18,297 --> 00:10:20,120
Nice girls, too.

316
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:22,342
You stood on the side of Route

317
00:10:22,342 --> 00:10:24,385
forty-two with your thumb out,

318
00:10:24,385 --> 00:10:26,564
and a farmer in a truck stopped,

319
00:10:26,564 --> 00:10:27,858
and you climbed in.

320
00:10:28,038 --> 00:10:29,524
I did it twice.

321
00:10:29,704 --> 00:10:32,223
Once to get home from Lake Erie

322
00:10:32,223 --> 00:10:34,661
when our ride left without us.

323
00:10:34,841 --> 00:10:36,502
I'm not proud of it.

324
00:10:36,682 --> 00:10:38,872
But I'd be lying if I said

325
00:10:38,872 --> 00:10:39,967
I was scared.

326
00:10:40,317 --> 00:10:42,833
And there was no drinking and driving

327
00:10:42,833 --> 00:10:44,125
law worth anything.

328
00:10:44,305 --> 00:10:46,644
The men drank three beers at the

329
00:10:46,644 --> 00:10:48,252
Legion and drove home.

330
00:10:48,432 --> 00:10:50,731
Our neighbor kept a bottle in his

331
00:10:50,731 --> 00:10:52,960
glove box and called it his road

332
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:53,239
map.

333
00:10:53,419 --> 00:10:55,369
Of course people died.

334
00:10:55,549 --> 00:10:56,373
Too many.

335
00:10:56,553 --> 00:10:59,558
It took mothers marching in the nineteen-eighties

336
00:10:59,558 --> 00:11:00,478
to change that.

337
00:11:00,658 --> 00:11:01,923
Good for them.

338
00:11:02,273 --> 00:11:03,594
So you're asking,

339
00:11:03,594 --> 00:11:06,470
Grandma, how did you live through all

340
00:11:06,470 --> 00:11:06,859
that?

341
00:11:07,039 --> 00:11:09,709
Well, honey, some of us didn't.

342
00:11:09,889 --> 00:11:11,979
I want to be honest with you.

343
00:11:12,159 --> 00:11:13,945
I knew a boy who died in

344
00:11:13,945 --> 00:11:14,690
the creek.

345
00:11:14,870 --> 00:11:16,951
I knew a girl who went through

346
00:11:16,951 --> 00:11:17,853
a windshield.

347
00:11:18,033 --> 00:11:20,289
We don't tell those stories at the

348
00:11:20,289 --> 00:11:20,820
reunion.

349
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,394
That's the price we paid for all

350
00:11:23,394 --> 00:11:24,367
that freedom.

351
00:11:24,717 --> 00:11:27,050
But I'll tell you what we had.

352
00:11:27,230 --> 00:11:28,542
We had nerve.

353
00:11:28,722 --> 00:11:30,638
We fixed our own bikes.

354
00:11:30,818 --> 00:11:33,000
We settled our own fights.

355
00:11:33,180 --> 00:11:35,667
We knew every family on six blocks,

356
00:11:35,667 --> 00:11:36,875
and they knew us,

357
00:11:36,875 --> 00:11:39,077
and if I did something wrong on

358
00:11:39,077 --> 00:11:41,918
Elm Street, Mother heard about it before

359
00:11:41,918 --> 00:11:43,339
I got home on Maple.

360
00:11:43,519 --> 00:11:45,187
Nobody was watching us,

361
00:11:45,187 --> 00:11:47,362
and everybody was watching us.

362
00:11:47,542 --> 00:11:49,430
I don't know how else to say

363
00:11:49,430 --> 00:11:49,632
it.

364
00:11:49,982 --> 00:11:52,574
I'm glad you have seat belts,

365
00:11:52,574 --> 00:11:53,558
sweetheart.

366
00:11:53,738 --> 00:11:56,837
I'm glad nobody gives you pep pills.

367
00:11:57,017 --> 00:11:58,167
I really am.

368
00:11:58,347 --> 00:12:00,848
But sometimes I wish you could run

369
00:12:00,848 --> 00:12:02,981
behind a truck in the summer,

370
00:12:02,981 --> 00:12:04,379
without the poison,

371
00:12:04,379 --> 00:12:06,659
just once, and feel what it was

372
00:12:06,659 --> 00:12:08,204
like to be that free.

373
00:12:08,384 --> 00:12:10,415
Nobody knew where we were.

374
00:12:10,595 --> 00:12:12,058
And we were fine.

375
00:12:12,238 --> 00:12:13,353
Mostly.

376
00:12:13,703 --> 00:12:16,756
Now, which one surprised you most?

377
00:12:16,936 --> 00:12:19,379
Tell me, and tell me which ones

378
00:12:19,379 --> 00:12:22,137
your friends' grandparents did too.

379
00:12:22,317 --> 00:12:24,349
I bet there are some I forgot.

380
00:12:24,529 --> 00:12:26,042
And come back tomorrow,

381
00:12:26,042 --> 00:12:28,475
because I'll tell you about the store

382
00:12:28,475 --> 00:12:30,119
where I had my first job,

383
00:12:30,119 --> 00:12:32,421
the one with the lunch counter that

384
00:12:32,421 --> 00:12:33,736
isn't there anymore.

385
00:12:33,916 --> 00:12:35,936
Goodnight, sweetheart.
